# Day29 – MVC Architecture

## Overview
Today I learned about MVC (Model-View-Controller) Architecture in Java Web Development. MVC is a design pattern used to separate an application into three main components: Model, View, and Controller.

## 1. What is MVC Architecture?
MVC stands for Model-View-Controller. It is a software design pattern that separates application logic, user interface, and request handling.

It makes applications easier to develop, maintain, test, and manage.

## 2. Components of MVC

### Model
- Represents application data and business-related information.
- Handles data and interacts with the database through the DAO layer.
- Example: Employee.java

### View
- Represents the user interface.
- Displays data to the user.
- In Java web applications, JSP is commonly used as the View.
- Example: login.jsp, dashboard.jsp, employee-list.jsp

### Controller
- Handles incoming HTTP requests.
- Communicates with the Model or Service layer.
- Selects the appropriate View and sends responses.
- In Java web applications, Servlets are commonly used as Controllers.
- Example: EmployeeServlet.java

## 3. Working of MVC Architecture

The basic request flow is:

Browser  
↓  
Controller (Servlet)  
↓  
Service Layer  
↓  
DAO Layer  
↓  
Database  

The response travels back through the Controller to the View (JSP), which displays the result to the user.

## 4. Advantages of MVC Architecture

- Separation of concerns.
- Easy maintenance and code management.
- Code reusability.
- Easier testing and debugging.
- Supports teamwork and parallel development.
- Makes applications more organized and scalable.

## 5. MVC in Java Web Development

In Java web applications:

| MVC Component | Java Technology |
|---|---|
| Model | Java Classes |
| View | JSP, HTML, CSS |
| Controller | Servlet |
| Database Access | JDBC, DAO |
| Business Logic | Service Layer |

## 6. MVC vs Three-Tier Architecture

| MVC Architecture | Three-Tier Architecture |
|---|---|
| Divides an application into Model, View, and Controller. | Divides an application into Presentation, Business, and Data layers. |
| Focuses on separating UI, data, and request handling. | Focuses on separating application responsibilities into layers. |
| Commonly used in web application design. | Commonly used in enterprise application architecture. |

## Conclusion

MVC Architecture helps organize Java web applications by separating data, user interface, and request-handling logic. It makes applications easier to maintain, understand, test, and extend.

**Technologies Learned:** Java, Servlet, JSP, JDBC, MVC Architecture, DAO Pattern.

